<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Reproduction for PR #4246 (skip writes and still shut down after failed DB
  manager startup) — https://github.com/apache/logging-log4j2/pull/4246
  and for the underlying issue #4241.

  Why JPA rather than Cassandra or NoSQL:

    CassandraManager   startupInternal CAN fail, writeInternal has NO guard   -> NPEs (the bug)
    JpaDatabaseManager startupInternal CAN fail, writeInternal HAS a guard    -> throws, correctly
    NoSqlDatabaseManager  startupInternal is empty, so it cannot fail this way
    JdbcDatabaseManager   startupInternal is empty, so it cannot fail this way

  JPA is the only manager that both fails startup for real WITHOUT any external
  service being down, and already handles the aftermath correctly. That makes it
  the vehicle for showing what PR #4246 would take away.

  persistenceUnitName is deliberately a name that does not exist in
  apps/jpa/src/main/resources/META-INF/persistence.xml. Persistence
  .createEntityManagerFactory throws, AbstractDatabaseManager.startup() catches
  it and leaves running=false.

  What happens next, on every released 2.x:

    write() -> writeThrough() -> connectAndStart()/writeInternal()
    JpaDatabaseManager.writeInternal:
        if (!isRunning() || entityManagerFactory == null || ...)
            throw new AppenderLoggingException(
                "Cannot write logging event; JPA manager not connected to the database.");

  ignoreExceptions="false" is the whole point of this config: it is what turns
  that AppenderLoggingException into something the APPLICATION sees, on the
  calling thread, rather than a StatusLogger line nobody reads.

  After PR #4246, AbstractDatabaseManager.write() returns early on !isRunning(),
  so writeInternal is never reached, the exception is never constructed, and the
  application is never told. One StatusLogger warning, then silence.

  Usable only with the jpa app, which owns the entity and the persistence unit.
-->
<Configuration status="WARN" name="repro-jpa-failed-startup">

  <Appenders>
    <Console name="Console" target="SYSTEM_OUT">
      <PatternLayout pattern="%d{HH:mm:ss.SSS} %-5level %logger{1.} - %msg%n"/>
    </Console>

    <!-- "benchLoggingDoesNotExist" is not declared in persistence.xml. That is
         the failure being induced, and it is induced inside Log4j's own startup
         path rather than by taking a database down. -->
    <JPA name="Jpa"
         persistenceUnitName="benchLoggingDoesNotExist"
         entityClassName="org.apache.logging.bench.jpa.BenchLogEntity"
         bufferSize="0"
         ignoreExceptions="false"/>
  </Appenders>

  <Loggers>
    <Logger name="org.apache.logging.bench" level="TRACE"/>
    <Root level="INFO">
      <AppenderRef ref="Console"/>
      <AppenderRef ref="Jpa"/>
    </Root>
  </Loggers>

</Configuration>
